$ goodfetch-collie events --help
Calls the catalog's `events` operations

Usage: goodfetch-collie events [OPTIONS] <COMMAND>

Commands:
  subscribe  Subscribes to the engine's events. The call stays in flight, sending its events in `$/events` batches, never more than the credits granted with `$/credit`, until it is cancelled. A lossless event is never dropped: while the subscription's queue is full, its producer waits. A telemetry event is coalesced with the unsent one of the same kind and scope, and counts it as `dropped`. Given `after`, it resumes after that position, or starts with `events.resync` when the engine no longer holds it

Options:
      --color <WHEN>  When to colour human output; `auto` colours on a terminal unless `NO_COLOR` is set and not empty [default: auto] [possible values: auto, always, never]
  -h, --help          Print help

$ goodfetch-collie events subscribe --help
Subscribes to the engine's events. The call stays in flight, sending its events in `$/events` batches, never more than the credits granted with `$/credit`, until it is cancelled. A lossless event is never dropped: while the subscription's queue is full, its producer waits. A telemetry event is coalesced with the unsent one of the same kind and scope, and counts it as `dropped`. Given `after`, it resumes after that position, or starts with `events.resync` when the engine no longer holds it

Usage: goodfetch-collie events subscribe [OPTIONS] --follow

Options:
      --follow         Prints what the call sends as it arrives, until it ends
      --after <AFTER>  The position of the last event the Client read: the subscription resumes after it, or starts with `events.resync` when the engine no longer holds it. Absent, it starts with the next event
      --json           Prints what the call sends as one JSON object per line
      --color <WHEN>   When to colour human output; `auto` colours on a terminal unless `NO_COLOR` is set and not empty [default: auto] [possible values: auto, always, never]
  -h, --help           Print help

$ goodfetch-collie run --help
Calls the catalog's `run` operations

Usage: goodfetch-collie run [OPTIONS] <COMMAND>

Commands:
  new-page    Opens a page in the Run's default Session, which the first call opens with the Run's settings and every later call shares; `page.session()` reaches it. The first page launches the Run's browser, so a missing browser build raises its code from this call
  start       Starts a Run its program drives, and answers once it is started. A Run ID names the Run for good: a start with an existing Run ID returns that Run as it is when the caller and the inputs match, and fails with `run_conflict` otherwise
  finish      Lets a Run finish and answers its Run report: a Run its program drives ends at once, `finished` and `complete`; a crawl ends once its work items are done. Cancelling the call ends only the wait
  abort       Stops a Run at its next safe point, `stopped` and `incomplete`: no attempt in flight commits, and its work items return to pending. Answers its Run report; an ended Run's report is answered as it is. A Run whose engine died is stopped by this engine, which takes its writer role at the next run epoch; a Run another engine writes is stopped by that engine, the owner the Run index names
  checkpoint  Writes a Checkpoint of a live Run now, through SQLite's online backup, where `location` says or where the engine keeps the Run's Checkpoints, and answers it once its file is complete, with its size, digest, run epoch and journal position. A Checkpoint the engine cannot write fails with `file_write_failed` and leaves no file
  pause       Pauses a live Run: checkpoints it, then stops it at its next safe point, `stopped` with the stop reason `pause` and resumable by its Run ID with `run.resume`, after a restart too. Answers its Run report, which names the Checkpoint
  migrate     Migrates a live Run: checkpoints it where `location` says or where the engine keeps its Checkpoints, then stops it at its next safe point, `stopped` with the stop reason `migrate`, to be resumed from that Checkpoint on another machine. Answers its Run report, which names the Checkpoint
  resume      Resumes an interrupted Run from its committed state, its run file on this machine or the Checkpoint `checkpoint` names: it takes the Run's writer lock and the next run epoch, so every write of an earlier writer is refused, and redoes the work since that state; its recorded Clean-up rules digest is kept. A live Run is answered as it is, `existing`; a finished one as it is. A Run bound to another machine fails with `session_bound_to_machine`, and a Checkpoint of a format version this engine cannot read with `format_version_unsupported`

Options:
      --color <WHEN>  When to colour human output; `auto` colours on a terminal unless `NO_COLOR` is set and not empty [default: auto] [possible values: auto, always, never]
  -h, --help          Print help

$ goodfetch-collie run new-page --help
