$ goodfetch-collie run resume --help
Resumes an interrupted Run from its committed state, its run file on this machine or the Checkpoint `checkpoint` names: it takes the Run's writer lock and the next run epoch, so every write of an earlier writer is refused, and redoes the work since that state; its recorded Clean-up rules digest is kept. A live Run is answered as it is, `existing`; a finished one as it is. A Run bound to another machine fails with `session_bound_to_machine`, and a Checkpoint of a format version this engine cannot read with `format_version_unsupported`

Usage: goodfetch-collie run resume [OPTIONS] <RUN_ID>

Arguments:
  <RUN_ID>  The Run's ID

Options:
      --checkpoint <CHECKPOINT>  A Checkpoint to resume from, a path on the engine's machine, which becomes the Run's state in this engine's run folder; by default the Run resumes from its run file on this machine
      --project <PROJECT>        The project configuration the Client found, layer 3 (ADR 0083)
      --set <KEY=VALUE>          Sets a setting for this run, as `key=value`, the value read as JSON when it parses as JSON and as text otherwise, a duration as milliseconds or as `45s`, `2m` or `1h`; repeatable
      --settings <FILE|->        Reads settings for this run from a JSON object, nested by key, in FILE, or from standard input for `-`; `--set` beats it
      --json                     Prints the result as JSON
      --color <WHEN>             When to colour human output; `auto` colours on a terminal unless `NO_COLOR` is set and not empty [default: auto] [possible values: auto, always, never]
  -h, --help                     Print help

$ goodfetch-collie page --help
Calls the catalog's `page` operations

Usage: goodfetch-collie page [OPTIONS] <COMMAND>

Commands:
  goto        Navigates the page to a URL and waits for its `load` event, answering the document's response: a 404 or a 500 is a response, a value. A navigation that reaches no document raises `navigation_failed`, and one another navigation supersedes before it committed `navigation_interrupted`
  reload      Loads the page's document again and waits for its `load` event, answering the document's response: a 404 or a 500 is a response, a value. A navigation that reaches no document raises `navigation_failed`, and one another navigation supersedes before it committed `navigation_interrupted`
  go-back     Goes back one entry in the page's history and waits for its `load` event, answering the document's response, or none when there is no entry to go back to. The back/forward cache is off, so the document always loads again (ADR 0121)
  go-forward  Goes forward one entry in the page's history and waits for its `load` event, answering the document's response, or none when there is no entry to go forward to. The back/forward cache is off, so the document always loads again (ADR 0121)
  formats     Reads the formats a request names from the page's current document, each read from one serialization of it, and answers only those, with the document's URL, its base URL and whether it has main content. A document with no main content answers empty formats and `main_content: "none"`, a value. It needs neither layout nor pixels, so every tier serves it
  body        Reads a page body by its handle, for a Client that cannot read the handle's file: sends the body in `$/chunk` notifications of at most 64 KiB, one per credit granted with `$/credit`, then answers its size

Options:
      --color <WHEN>  When to colour human output; `auto` colours on a terminal unless `NO_COLOR` is set and not empty [default: auto] [possible values: auto, always, never]
  -h, --help          Print help

$ goodfetch-collie page goto --help
Navigates the page to a URL and waits for its `load` event, answering the document's response: a 404 or a 500 is a response, a value. A navigation that reaches no document raises `navigation_failed`, and one another navigation supersedes before it committed `navigation_interrupted`

Usage: goodfetch-collie page goto [OPTIONS] --url <URL> <PAGE_ID>

Arguments:
  <PAGE_ID>  The page

Options:
      --url <URL>     The URL
      --json          Prints the result as JSON
      --color <WHEN>  When to colour human output; `auto` colours on a terminal unless `NO_COLOR` is set and not empty [default: auto] [possible values: auto, always, never]
  -h, --help          Print help

$ goodfetch-collie page reload --help
Loads the page's document again and waits for its `load` event, answering the document's response: a 404 or a 500 is a response, a value. A navigation that reaches no document raises `navigation_failed`, and one another navigation supersedes before it committed `navigation_interrupted`

Usage: goodfetch-collie page reload [OPTIONS] <PAGE_ID>

Arguments:
  <PAGE_ID>  The page

Options:
      --json          Prints the result as JSON
      --color <WHEN>  When to colour human output; `auto` colours on a terminal unless `NO_COLOR` is set and not empty [default: auto] [possible values: auto, always, never]
  -h, --help          Print help

$ goodfetch-collie page go-back --help
Goes back one entry in the page's history and waits for its `load` event, answering the document's response, or none when there is no entry to go back to. The back/forward cache is off, so the document always loads again (ADR 0121)

Usage: goodfetch-collie page go-back [OPTIONS] <PAGE_ID>

Arguments:
  <PAGE_ID>  The page

Options:
      --json          Prints the result as JSON
      --color <WHEN>  When to colour human output; `auto` colours on a terminal unless `NO_COLOR` is set and not empty [default: auto] [possible values: auto, always, never]
  -h, --help          Print help

$ goodfetch-collie page go-forward --help
Goes forward one entry in the page's history and waits for its `load` event, answering the document's response, or none when there is no entry to go forward to. The back/forward cache is off, so the document always loads again (ADR 0121)

Usage: goodfetch-collie page go-forward [OPTIONS] <PAGE_ID>

Arguments:
  <PAGE_ID>  The page

Options:
      --json          Prints the result as JSON
      --color <WHEN>  When to colour human output; `auto` colours on a terminal unless `NO_COLOR` is set and not empty [default: auto] [possible values: auto, always, never]
  -h, --help          Print help

$ goodfetch-collie page formats --help
Reads the formats a request names from the page's current document, each read from one serialization of it, and answers only those, with the document's URL, its base URL and whether it has main content. A document with no main content answers empty formats and `main_content: "none"`, a value. It needs neither layout nor pixels, so every tier serves it

Usage: goodfetch-collie page formats [OPTIONS] --formats <FORMATS> <PAGE_ID>

Arguments:
  <PAGE_ID>  The page

Options:
      --formats <FORMATS>  The formats
      --json               Prints the result as JSON
      --color <WHEN>       When to colour human output; `auto` colours on a terminal unless `NO_COLOR` is set and not empty [default: auto] [possible values: auto, always, never]
  -h, --help               Print help

$ goodfetch-collie page body --help
Reads a page body by its handle, for a Client that cannot read the handle's file: sends the body in `$/chunk` notifications of at most 64 KiB, one per credit granted with `$/credit`, then answers its size

Usage: goodfetch-collie page body [OPTIONS] --follow --body <BODY> --run-id <RUN_ID>

Options:
      --follow           Prints what the call sends as it arrives, until it ends
      --body <BODY>      Its ID
      --run-id <RUN_ID>  The Run whose folder holds it
      --json             Prints what the call sends as one JSON object per line
      --color <WHEN>     When to colour human output; `auto` colours on a terminal unless `NO_COLOR` is set and not empty [default: auto] [possible values: auto, always, never]
  -h, --help             Print help

$ goodfetch-collie locator --help
Calls the catalog's `locator` operations

Usage: goodfetch-collie locator [OPTIONS] <COMMAND>

Commands:
  click  Clicks the element the locator matches once it is unique, visible, enabled, stable and receiving the click (the guard, ADR 0019); a click that lands on another element is cancelled and sent again, until `timeout.action` passes
  fill   Replaces the text of the field the locator matches, once it is unique, visible, enabled and editable, with text the page receives as typed input, until `timeout.action` passes
  press  Focuses the element the locator matches, once it is unique, visible and enabled, and presses a key or a combination on it as a keyboard does, until `timeout.action` passes

Options:
      --color <WHEN>  When to colour human output; `auto` colours on a terminal unless `NO_COLOR` is set and not empty [default: auto] [possible values: auto, always, never]
  -h, --help          Print help

